ich often includes lighter layers.\n\n---\n\n✅ **Visible trees with leaves (green or changing colors)**  \n→ **Yes, but not indicative of autumn.**  \nPalm trees and other green-leaved trees are visible (e.g., Frame 1, 3, 4), but they are **evergreen** (palm trees don’t change color or drop leaves in autumn). No evidence of deciduous trees with changing or falling leaves.\n\n---\n\n❌ **Visible trees without leaves (bare branches)**  \n→ **No.**  \nNo bare branches or leafless trees are visible in any frame.\n\n---\n\n❌ **People using umbrellas or rain gear**  \n→ **No.**  \nNo umbrellas, raincoats, or wet surfaces visible. Weather appears dry.\n\n---\n\n❌ **People showing visible breath in cold air**  \n→ **No.**  \nNo visible breath, which might suggest it’s not extremely cold — but this doesn’t override the clothing evidence.\n\n---\n\n### Conclusion:\n\nWhile the clothing suggests **cool/cold weather**, the **lack of autumn-specific indicators** (changing leaf colors, transitional layers, bare trees) and the **dominance of heavy jackets/coats** point more toward **winter** or **late autumn transitioning to winter**.\n\nIn many classification systems, “Autumn” (0) implies a season with mild temperatures and changing foliage — which is **not supported** here.\n\n---\n\n### Final Verdict:\n**The answer '0' (Autumn) is INCORRECT.**  \nThe visual evidence better supports **Winter (1)** or at least **Late Autumn/Early Winter**, but not classic autumn.\n\n✅ **Correct answer should be: 1 (Winter)** — based on clothing and absence of autumnal foliage cues."}], 'formatted_responses': ['\n=== Batch 0-30 (30 frames) ===\nFrames: /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/wgBlACG927Y/frames/frame_0008.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/wgBlACG927Y/frames/frame_0009.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/wgBlACG927Y/frames/frame_0010.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/wgBlACG927Y/frames/frame_0011.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/wgBlACG927Y/frame